# A MongoDB client written in Heroes: it speaks MongoDB's wire protocol
# (OP_MSG) over a TCP connection of `net` or a TLS connection of `tls`, and
# links no library. Documents are `bson.Document`s; a filter, an update, a
# projection, a sort or a pipeline is one too, written in code or read from
# Extended JSON by `bson.parse_json`. It connects by a connection string
# (`mongodb://` and `mongodb+srv://`, whose servers and options `dns` asks),
# logs in with SCRAM-SHA-256 or SCRAM-SHA-1 (the server tells which it holds
# for the user) or PLAIN, and runs every command: CRUD with cursors, bulk
# writes, aggregation, counts and `distinct`, indexes, change streams, and
# transactions on a replica set, with the read and write concerns and the
# read preference a program names.
#
# **One connection, one server, one thread.** A `Client` is one connection to
# the one server its read preference chose when it connected, carrying one
# logical session; a program that works on several threads opens a client in
# each, since threads share nothing in this language, and there is no pool to
# share. Every call works on the program's own place through `@`. Reads are
# retried once, and writes that may be retried once with the same
# transaction number, on a new connection, where the first attempt lost its
# connection or met a primary that stepped down, as the drivers'
# specifications ask; a server that is no longer suitable is not followed by
# a monitor.
#
# It does not compress (OP_COMPRESSED), encrypt fields on the client
# (libmongocrypt), log in by Kerberos, X.509, AWS or OIDC, connect through a
# load balancer, or report events to a monitor; the ledger says why of each.
#
# ## Failures
#
# A call fails with the codes below. A failure the server reports keeps the
# server's own code, its name and its labels in brackets before its words,
# `[11000 DuplicateKey] E11000 duplicate key error ...`, `[251
# NoSuchTransaction; TransientTransactionError] ...`, and `server_code_of`
# and `has_label` read them back. The server's code chooses Rebel's:
# `exists` for a duplicate key and a namespace or an index that exists;
# `permission` for a login refused and a command the user may not run;
# `not_found` for a namespace, an index, a cursor or a transaction that is
# not there; `timeout` for a time limit passed and a write concern that
# waited past its time; `limit` for a document past 16 MiB and a stage past
# its memory; `invalid` for a command, a filter or a value refused as
# written; `unsupported` for a command the server does not know or does not
# run here; and `failed` for every other, a primary that stepped down and a
# write conflict among them. A connection lost, or bytes that are no OP_MSG,
# end the client: every later call fails `closed`.

use bson
use mongodb/internal/commands
use mongodb/internal/conn
use mongodb/internal/cursors
use mongodb/internal/dial
use mongodb/internal/ops
use mongodb/internal/replies
use mongodb/internal/session
use mongodb/internal/uri
use mongodb/internal/writes
use os

## Codes

# The code of an argument refused, a connection string that is none, a reply
# that is not of the kind asked, and what the server refuses as written.
constant INVALID: str
    "invalid"

# The code of a document `find_one` and its kin found none of, a cursor with
# no more documents, no server that suits the read preference, and what the
# server says is not there.
constant NOT_FOUND: str
    "not_found"

# The code of a duplicate key, and of a collection or an index that exists.
constant EXISTS: str
    "exists"

# The code of a login refused and of a command the user may not run.
constant PERMISSION: str
    "permission"

# The code of a client that is closed, or whose connection the server or the
# network closed while the call waited.
constant CLOSED: str
    "closed"

# The code of a call whose time ran out: a socket's timeout, a `maxTimeMS`,
# a write concern's `wtimeout`, a change stream's wait.
constant TIMEOUT: str
    "timeout"

# The code of a call that ended because the program was asked to stop.
constant STOPPED: str
    "stopped"

# The code of a document or a message past what the server takes, and of a
# stage past its memory.
constant LIMIT: str
    "limit"

# The code of what this client or the server does not do: an option, a
# mechanism, a command, a transaction on a standalone server.
constant UNSUPPORTED: str
    "unsupported"

# The code of every other failure the server reports.
constant FAILED: str
    "failed"

# The port MongoDB listens on unless told another.
constant DEFAULT_PORT: i64
    27017

# The label of a failure after which the whole transaction may be run again.
constant LABEL_TRANSIENT_TRANSACTION_ERROR: str
    "TransientTransactionError"

# The label of a commit whose outcome is unknown, which may be committed
# again.
constant LABEL_UNKNOWN_TRANSACTION_COMMIT_RESULT: str
    "UnknownTransactionCommitResult"

# The label of a write the server says may be sent again.
constant LABEL_RETRYABLE_WRITE_ERROR: str
    "RetryableWriteError"

## Connecting

# How the connection is secured.
variant Security
    # Nothing encrypted: whoever sits between the two ends reads every
    # document. SCRAM's login sends no password either way. `mongodb://`'s
    # default.
    plain
    # TLS from the first byte, the server's certificate checked for its
    # authority and its name: `tls=true`, and `mongodb+srv://`'s default.
    checked
        # The PEM of the only authorities trusted; "" for the system's own
        # store.
        roots: str

# How the user logs in.
variant Mechanism
    # SCRAM-SHA-256 where the server holds it for the user, SCRAM-SHA-1
    # otherwise, as the server's `hello` tells.
    negotiated
    # SCRAM-SHA-1, the password hashed as MongoDB's old scheme did.
    scram_sha_1
    # SCRAM-SHA-256, the password prepared by SASLprep.
    scram_sha_256
    # SASL PLAIN, for LDAP's proxy on `$external`: the password is sent as it
    # is, so only over TLS.
    sasl_plain

# Which server of a replica set the client connects to and reads from.
variant ReadPreference
    # The primary alone.
    primary
    # The primary, else a secondary.
    primary_preferred
    # A secondary alone.
    secondary
    # A secondary, else the primary.
    secondary_preferred
    # Any member that answers.
    nearest

# Whether a write waits for the server's journal.
variant Journal
    # As the server's default says.
    server_default
    # A write is acknowledged once in the journal: `j: true`.
    required
    # It is not waited for: `j: false`.
    not_required

# How a write is acknowledged. `w` "" with `server_default` and no time asks
# the server's own default.
record WriteConcern
    # "" for the server's default; "majority"; a number of members, "0" (no
    # acknowledgement), "1", "2", ...; or the name of a tag set.
    w: str
    # Whether the journal is waited for.
    journal: Journal
    # How long a write waits for its members, in milliseconds; 0 for no
    # limit.
    timeout_ms: i64

# One server's name and port.
record Host
    # A name or an address, without brackets: `localhost`, `::1`.
    name: str
    # The TCP port, 1 to 65535.
    port: i64

# Where the servers are, how to log in, and how commands are sent. `options`
# makes one; a program changes the fields it needs with `@`, or `parse_url`
# reads one from a connection string.
record Options
    # The servers tried, in order; [] where `srv_name` names them.
    hosts: [Host]
    # The name whose SRV records name the servers, as `mongodb+srv://` gives
    # it; "" for `hosts`.
    srv_name: str
    # The service of the SRV records, `mongodb` by default.
    srv_service_name: str
    # The most servers taken from the SRV records, 0 for all.
    srv_max_hosts: i64
    # The user, "" for none: no login.
    username: str
    # The password.
    password: str
    # The database the user is defined in; "" for `database`, or `admin`
    # where that is "" too, or `$external` for PLAIN.
    auth_source: str
    # How the user logs in.
    mechanism: Mechanism
    # The database a connection string names, "" for none; read by nothing
    # but `auth_source`'s default.
    database: str
    # The replica set's name, "" for any: a server of another set is refused.
    replica_set: str
    # Whether the client connects to the one host as it is, whatever it is,
    # without looking for the read preference's server.
    direct: bool
    # How the connection is secured.
    security: Security
    # The application's name the server logs, "" for none.
    app_name: str
    # How long a TCP connection may take to be made, in milliseconds.
    connect_timeout_ms: i64
    # How long each command may wait for its reply, in milliseconds; 0 for
    # no limit.
    socket_timeout_ms: i64
    # Which server the client connects to and reads from.
    read_preference: ReadPreference
    # The read concern's level of reads: "" for the server's default,
    # `local`, `available`, `majority`, `linearizable`, `snapshot`.
    read_concern: str
    # The write concern of writes.
    write_concern: WriteConcern
    # Whether a write that may be retried is sent again once after a lost
    # connection or a stepped-down primary.
    retry_writes: bool
    # Whether a read is sent again once after the same.
    retry_reads: bool
    # The stable API version every command declares, "" for none, "1".
    server_api: str

# The options of a connection to `host` at `DEFAULT_PORT`, as the drivers
# default them: no user, plain TCP, the primary, the server's concerns, reads
# and writes retried, a TCP connection given 10 seconds and no time limit on
# a reply.
#
# - `host`: the server's name or address, without brackets.
#
# See also `parse_url`, `connect`.
function options(host: str) -> Options
    return Options(
        hosts: [Host(name: host, port: DEFAULT_PORT)],
        srv_name: "",
        srv_service_name: "mongodb",
        srv_max_hosts: 0,
        username: "",
        password: "",
        auth_source: "",
        mechanism: .negotiated,
        database: "",
        replica_set: "",
        direct: false,
        security: .plain,
        app_name: "",
        connect_timeout_ms: 10000,
        socket_timeout_ms: 0,
        read_preference: .primary,
        read_concern: "",
        write_concern: WriteConcern(w: "", journal: .server_default, timeout_ms: 0),
        retry_writes: true,
        retry_reads: true,
        server_api: ""
    )

# The options a connection string names, as the drivers' URI specification
# reads one: `mongodb://user:password@host1:port1,host2/database?options`
# or `mongodb+srv://user:password@cluster.example.com/database?options`.
#
# - `text`: the connection string. Its user and password are percent-decoded
#   and must hold no unescaped `:` or `@`; a host in brackets is an IPv6
#   address; its options' names are read in any case: `authSource`,
#   `authMechanism`, `replicaSet`, `directConnection`, `tls` (and `ssl`),
#   `tlsCAFile` (whose file is read), `appName`, `connectTimeoutMS`,
#   `socketTimeoutMS` and `timeoutMS`, `readPreference`, `readConcernLevel`,
#   `w`, `journal`, `wtimeoutMS`, `retryWrites`, `retryReads`,
#   `srvServiceName`, `srvMaxHosts`; the options of a pool, a monitor and
#   server selection's timing are read and have no effect here.
#   `mongodb+srv://` turns TLS on unless `tls=false` says otherwise; `connect`
#   asks DNS for its servers and its TXT record's options.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `text` is no connection string, names an empty user,
# a port outside 1 to 65535, an option this client knows nothing of, or a
# value an option does not take; `unsupported` where it asks what this
# client does not do (`tlsInsecure=true`, a client certificate,
# `loadBalanced=true`, `compressors`, a Unix socket, another mechanism);
# fails where `read_file` fails on `tlsCAFile`, with its code. See also
# `options`, `connect`.
function parse_url(text: str) -> Options?
    s = uri.parse(text)?
    hosts: [Host] @= []

    for a in s.hosts
        hosts @ hosts.push(Host(name: a.host, port: a.port))

    mechanism: Mechanism = if s.mechanism == "SCRAM-SHA-1"
        .scram_sha_1
    else if s.mechanism == "SCRAM-SHA-256"
        .scram_sha_256
    else if s.mechanism == "PLAIN"
        .sasl_plain
    else
        .negotiated

    preference: ReadPreference = if s.read_preference == "primaryPreferred"
        .primary_preferred
    else if s.read_preference == "secondary"
        .secondary
    else if s.read_preference == "secondaryPreferred"
        .secondary_preferred
    else if s.read_preference == "nearest"
        .nearest
    else
        .primary

    journal: Journal = if s.journal == "true"
        .required
    else if s.journal == "false"
        .not_required
    else
        .server_default

    security: Security = if s.tls
        .checked(roots: s.tls_roots)
    else
        .plain
    return ok(
        Options(hosts: hosts, srv_name: s.srv_name, srv_service_name: s.srv_service_name, srv_max_hosts: s.srv_max_hosts, username: s.username, password: s.password, auth_source: s.auth_source, mechanism: mechanism, database: s.database, replica_set: s.replica_set, direct: s.direct, security: security, app_name: s.app_name, connect_timeout_ms: s.connect_timeout_ms, socket_timeout_ms: s.socket_timeout_ms, read_preference: preference, read_concern: s.read_concern, write_concern: WriteConcern(w: s.w, journal: journal, timeout_ms: s.wtimeout_ms), retry_writes: s.retry_writes, retry_reads: s.retry_reads, server_api: s.server_api)
    )

# A connection to a MongoDB server, which `connect` makes and `close` ends.
# Its one field is the conversation's private state: the connection, what the
# server said of itself, the logical session and its transaction. A program
# keeps one place per client and lends it to every call with `@`.
record Client
    # The conversation's state, which only this package reads and writes.
    state: session.State

# A client of the server `opts` choose: the SRV name resolved by the
# system's resolver where one is named, then each host tried in order and
# greeted with `hello`, the first that suits the read preference kept (a
# server that is not names the one that is, which is tried next), and the
# user logged in.
#
# - `opts`: where the servers are and how to log in.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `opts` names no host or a port outside 1 to 65535;
# `not_found` where no server suits the read preference or the replica set's
# name; `permission` where the login is refused; and with the code of the
# last server's failure where none answers (`closed`, `timeout`,
# `not_found` for a name with no address, a TLS failure of `tls`). The
# client owes a `close`. See also `parse_url`, `close`.
function connect(opts: Options) -> Client?
    if opts.hosts.len() == 0 && opts.srv_name == ""
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: "the options name no host")
    addresses: [uri.Address] @= []

    for h in opts.hosts
        if h.port < 1 || h.port > 65535
            return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the port {h.port} of {h.name} is outside 1 to 65535")
        addresses @ addresses.push(uri.Address(host: h.name, port: h.port))

    mechanism = match opts.mechanism
        .negotiated    => ""
        .scram_sha_1   => "SCRAM-SHA-1"
        .scram_sha_256 => "SCRAM-SHA-256"
        .sasl_plain    => "PLAIN"

    preference = match opts.read_preference
        .primary             => "primary"
        .primary_preferred   => "primaryPreferred"
        .secondary           => "secondary"
        .secondary_preferred => "secondaryPreferred"
        .nearest             => "nearest"

    journal = match opts.write_concern.journal
        .server_default => ""
        .required       => "true"
        .not_required   => "false"

    secure = match opts.security
        .plain     => false
        .checked _ => true

    roots = match opts.security
        .plain     => ""
        .checked k => k.roots

    settings = uri.Settings(
        hosts: addresses,
        srv_name: opts.srv_name,
        srv_service_name: opts.srv_service_name,
        srv_max_hosts: opts.srv_max_hosts,
        username: opts.username,
        password: opts.password,
        auth_source: opts.auth_source,
        mechanism: mechanism,
        database: opts.database,
        replica_set: opts.replica_set,
        direct: opts.direct,
        tls: secure,
        tls_roots: roots,
        app_name: opts.app_name,
        connect_timeout_ms: opts.connect_timeout_ms,
        socket_timeout_ms: opts.socket_timeout_ms,
        read_preference: preference,
        read_concern: opts.read_concern,
        w: opts.write_concern.w,
        journal: journal,
        wtimeout_ms: opts.write_concern.timeout_ms,
        retry_writes: opts.retry_writes,
        retry_reads: opts.retry_reads,
        server_api: opts.server_api
    )
    return ok(Client(state: dial.select(settings)?))

# Ends `client`: its logical session is ended on the server, whatever it
# answers, and its connection closed.
#
# - `client`: the client, after which every call on it fails `closed`.
#
# Fails `closed` where it was closed already. See also `connect`.
function close(@client: Client) -> ()?
    if !client.state.conn.ended && client.state.sessions
        ids = bson.Document(
            fields: [bson.Field(name: "endSessions", value: .array(items: [.document(doc: client.state.lsid)]))]
        )

        match session.command(@client.state, database: "admin", body: ids, kind: session.NO_SESSION, txn: 0, sequences: [])
            .ok _  => _ = 0
            .err _ => _ = 0
    return conn.close(@client.state.conn)

# Whether `client` is closed, by `close` or by a connection lost.
#
# - `client`: the client.
function is_closed(client: Client) -> bool
    return client.state.conn.ended

# Makes every later command of `client` wait at most `ms` milliseconds for
# its reply, failing `timeout`; 0 takes the limit away.
#
# - `client`: the client, whose `socket_timeout_ms` this changes.
# - `ms`: the milliseconds, 0 or more.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `ms` is below 0. A command whose time runs out ends
# the client, since its reply may still come. See also `Options`.
function set_timeout(@client: Client, ms: i64) -> ()?
    if ms < 0
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"a timeout of {ms} ms")
    client.state.settings.socket_timeout_ms @ ms
    return ok()

# What `server_of` answers: what the server the client stands on said of
# itself.
record ServerInfo
    # The server, `host:port`.
    address: str
    # The replica set's name, "" for a standalone server or a mongos.
    replica_set: str
    # Whether it is the replica set's primary, a standalone, or a mongos:
    # one that takes writes.
    is_writable: bool
    # Whether it is a mongos.
    is_mongos: bool
    # Whether it keeps logical sessions, which transactions and retried writes
    # need.
    has_sessions: bool
    # The highest wire version it speaks: 21 for MongoDB 7.0, 25 for 8.0.
    max_wire_version: i64
    # The most bytes of a document, of a message, and writes in one batch.
    max_document_size: i64
    max_message_size: i64
    max_write_batch_size: i64
    # Its whole `hello` reply.
    hello: bson.Document

# What the server `client` stands on said of itself when it connected.
#
# - `client`: the client.
function server_of(client: Client) -> ServerInfo
    s = client.state
    return ServerInfo(
        address: s.conn.target,
        replica_set: s.set_name,
        is_writable: s.is_primary,
        is_mongos: s.is_mongos,
        has_sessions: s.sessions,
        max_wire_version: s.max_wire,
        max_document_size: s.max_bson,
        max_message_size: s.max_message,
        max_write_batch_size: s.max_batch,
        hello: s.hello
    )

# Asks the server whether it answers: the command `ping`.
#
# - `client`: the client.
#
# Fails as every command fails. See also `run_command`.
function ping(@client: Client) -> ()?
    _ = run_command(@client, database: "admin", command: bson.Document(fields: [bson.Field(name: "ping", value: bson.from_int(1))]))?
    return ok()

## Commands

# The reply to `command`, run on `database` as it is, with what the protocol
# adds: the session, the transaction, the read preference, the cluster time.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `command`: the command, its name its first field: `{"buildInfo": 1}`.
#
# Fails with the code of the server's failure, and as every command fails.
# No read or write concern is added: the command carries its own. See also
# `run_cursor_command`.
function run_command(@client: Client, database: str, command: bson.Document) -> bson.Document?
    return session.command(@client.state, database: database, body: command, kind: session.PLAIN, txn: 0, sequences: [])

# The cursor a command answers with, `listCollections` or a command of a
# server's own: run on `database`, its first batch read.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `command`: the command, which answers a cursor.
#
# Fails `invalid` where the reply holds no cursor, and as `run_command`
# fails. See also `next_document`.
function run_cursor_command(@client: Client, database: str, command: bson.Document) -> Cursor?
    reply = run_command(@client, database: database, command: command)?
    return ok(
        Cursor(state: cursors.of_reply(reply, database: database, batch_size: 0, max_await_ms: 0, is_stream: false)?)
    )

# The server's plan for `command`, as `explain` answers it.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `command`: the command explained: a `find`, an `aggregate`, an
#   `update`...
# - `verbosity`: `queryPlanner`, `executionStats` or
#   `allPlansExecution`.
#
# Fails as `run_command` fails. See also `run_command`.
function explain(@client: Client, database: str, command: bson.Document, verbosity: str) -> bson.Document?
    body = bson.Document(
        fields: [bson.Field(name: "explain", value: .document(doc: command)), bson.Field(name: "verbosity", value: .text(v: verbosity))]
    )
    return run_command(@client, database: database, command: body)

## Databases and collections

# The names of the databases on the server, in its order.
#
# - `client`: the client.
#
# Fails `permission` where the user may not list them, and as every command
# fails. See also `list_databases`.
function list_database_names(@client: Client) -> [str]?
    names: [str] @= []

    for d in list_databases(@client, query: bson.Document(fields: []))?
        names @ names.push(bson.text_of(bson.member(d, name: "name")?)?)

    return ok(names)

# The databases on the server, each a document of its `name`, `sizeOnDisk`
# and `empty`.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `query`: which databases, a filter on those fields; empty for all.
#
# Fails as `list_database_names` fails. See also `list_database_names`.
function list_databases(@client: Client, query: bson.Document) -> [bson.Document]?
    fields: [bson.Field] @= [bson.Field(name: "listDatabases", value: bson.from_int(1))]

    if query.fields.len() > 0
        fields @ fields.push(bson.Field(name: "filter", value: .document(doc: query)))
    reply = ops.read(@client.state, database: "admin", body: bson.Document(fields: fields))?
    found: [bson.Document] @= []

    for d in bson.items_of(bson.member(reply, name: "databases")?)?
        found @ found.push(bson.document_of(d)?)

    return ok(found)

# Removes the database `database` and all it holds.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name; one that does not exist is no failure.
#
# Fails as every write fails. See also `drop_collection`.
function drop_database(@client: Client, database: str) -> ()?
    _ = ops.write(@client.state, database: database, body: bson.Document(fields: [bson.Field(name: "dropDatabase", value: bson.from_int(1))]), sequences: [], retryable: false)?
    return ok()

# The names of the collections and views of `database`.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
#
# Fails as `list_collections` fails. See also `list_collections`.
function list_collection_names(@client: Client, database: str) -> [str]?
    names: [str] @= []

    for c in list_collections(@client, database: database, query: bson.Document(fields: []))?
        names @ names.push(bson.text_of(bson.member(c, name: "name")?)?)

    return ok(names)

# The collections and views of `database`, each the server's document of
# its `name`, `type`, `options` and `info`.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `query`: a filter on those fields; empty for all.
#
# Fails as every read fails. See also `list_collection_names`.
function list_collections(@client: Client, database: str, query: bson.Document) -> [bson.Document]?
    body = bson.Document(
        fields: [bson.Field(name: "listCollections", value: bson.from_int(1)), bson.Field(name: "filter", value: .document(doc: query))]
    )
    reply = ops.read(@client.state, database: database, body: body)?
    c @= cursors.of_reply(reply, database: database, batch_size: 0, max_await_ms: 0, is_stream: false)?
    return cursors.read_all(s: @client.state, c: @c)

# Creates the collection `collection`, with the server's options.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the new collection's name.
# - `opts`: the fields `create` takes beside its name: `capped`, `size`,
#   `max`, `validator`, `timeseries`, `expireAfterSeconds`,
#   `clusteredIndex`, `collation`...; empty for none.
#
# Fails `exists` where a collection of that name exists with other options,
# and as every write fails; one with the same options is no failure, as
# MongoDB 7.0 answers. See also `create_view`, `drop_collection`.
function create_collection(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, opts: bson.Document) -> ()?
    body = commands.merged(
        base: bson.Document(fields: [bson.Field(name: "create", value: .text(v: collection))]),
        extra: opts
    )
    _ = ops.write(@client.state, database: database, body: body, sequences: [], retryable: false)?
    return ok()

# Creates the view `view` of the collection `source` through `pipeline`.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `view`: the view's name.
# - `source`: the collection or view it reads.
# - `pipeline`: the stages it applies.
#
# Fails `exists` where the name is taken, and as every write fails. See also
# `create_collection`.
function create_view(@client: Client, database: str, view: str, source: str, pipeline: [bson.Document]) -> ()?
    stages: [bson.Value] @= []

    for p in pipeline
        stages @ stages.push(.document(doc: p))

    body = bson.Document(
        fields: [bson.Field(name: "create", value: .text(v: view)), bson.Field(name: "viewOn", value: .text(v: source)), bson.Field(name: "pipeline", value: .array(items: stages))]
    )
    _ = ops.write(@client.state, database: database, body: body, sequences: [], retryable: false)?
    return ok()

# Removes the collection `collection`, its documents and its indexes.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name; one that does not exist is no
#   failure, as the drivers treat it.
#
# Fails as every write fails. See also `create_collection`.
function drop_collection(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str) -> ()?
    body = bson.Document(fields: [bson.Field(name: "drop", value: .text(v: collection))])

    return match ops.write(@client.state, database: database, body: body, sequences: [], retryable: false)
        .ok _ => ok()
        .err e =>
            if replies.code_of(e.msg).default(0) == 26
                return ok()
            fail(code: e.code, msg: e.msg)

# Gives the collection `from` the name `to`, in one database.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `from`: the collection's name.
# - `to`: its new name.
# - `drop_target`: whether a collection already named `to` is removed first;
#   otherwise it fails `exists`.
#
# Fails `not_found` where `from` does not exist, `exists` where `to` does
# and `drop_target` is false, and as every write fails.
function rename_collection(@client: Client, database: str, from: str, to: str, drop_target: bool) -> ()?
    body = bson.Document(
        fields: [bson.Field(name: "renameCollection", value: .text(v: database + "." + from)), bson.Field(name: "to", value: .text(v: database + "." + to)), bson.Field(name: "dropTarget", value: .boolean(v: drop_target))]
    )
    _ = ops.write(@client.state, database: "admin", body: body, sequences: [], retryable: false)?
    return ok()

## Writing

# Inserts `doc` into `collection`, with a new ObjectId as its `_id` where it
# has none, and answers its `_id`.
#
# - `client`: the client, whose maker of ids this advances.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name; one that does not exist is made.
# - `doc`: the document; an `_id` it lacks is put first.
#
# Fails `exists` where its `_id` or a unique index's key is taken, `limit`
# where it passes the server's 16 MiB, and as every write fails. Retried
# once as a retryable write. See also `insert_many`.
function insert_one(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, doc: bson.Document) -> bson.Value?
    stored = writes.with_id(s: @client.state, doc: doc)?
    _ = writes.send(@client.state, database: database, command: "insert", collection: collection, identifier: "documents", items: [stored], ordered: true, retryable: true, extra: bson.Document(fields: []))?
    return bson.member(stored, name: "_id")

# Inserts `docs` into `collection`, in batches the server takes, each with a
# new ObjectId where it has no `_id`, and answers their `_id`s in order.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `docs`: the documents; the empty list inserts none.
# - `ordered`: whether the first failure stops the rest; otherwise every
#   document is tried and the first failure reported at the end.
#
# Fails with the first write's failure, its index in `docs` and how many
# others failed in its message, and as `insert_one` fails. See also
# `insert_one`, `bulk_write`.
function insert_many(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, docs: [bson.Document], ordered: bool) -> [bson.Value]?
    stored: [bson.Document] @= []
    ids: [bson.Value] @= []

    for d in docs
        s = writes.with_id(s: @client.state, doc: d)?
        stored @ stored.push(s)
        ids @ ids.push(bson.member(s, name: "_id")?)

    if stored.len() == 0
        return ok(ids)
    _ = writes.send(@client.state, database: database, command: "insert", collection: collection, identifier: "documents", items: stored, ordered: ordered, retryable: true, extra: bson.Document(fields: []))?
    return ok(ids)

# How an update, a replacement or a delete is done. `write_options` answers
# the defaults; a program changes the fields it needs.
record WriteOptions
    # Whether a document is inserted where the filter matches none: an
    # update's or a replacement's upsert. A delete ignores it.
    upsert: bool
    # The filters naming which elements of an array an update's `$[name]`
    # changes; [] for none.
    array_filters: [bson.Document]
    # Fields joined to each statement: `collation`, `hint`; empty for none.
    statement: bson.Document
    # Fields joined to the command: `comment`, `let`,
    # `bypassDocumentValidation`, `writeConcern`; empty for none.
    command: bson.Document

# The options of a plain write: no upsert, no array filter, no field more.
function write_options() -> WriteOptions
    return WriteOptions(
        upsert: false,
        array_filters: [],
        statement: bson.Document(fields: []),
        command: bson.Document(fields: [])
    )

# What an update or a replacement answers.
record UpdateResult
    # The documents the filter matched.
    matched: i64
    # The documents changed; a document matched and left as it was is not.
    modified: i64
    # The `_id` of the document an upsert inserted, [] where none was.
    upserted_ids: [bson.Value]

# Updates the first document of `collection` that `query` matches.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `query`: the documents considered; empty for any.
# - `update`: a document of operators, `{"$set": {"a": 1}}`, or an array of
#   stages, `[{"$set": {"a": "$b"}}]`.
# - `opts`: the upsert, the array filters and the fields joined.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `update` is no document of operators nor an array of
# stages, and as every write fails. Retried once as a retryable write. See
# also `update_many`, `replace_one`.
function update_one(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, query: bson.Document, update: bson.Value, opts: WriteOptions) -> UpdateResult?
    statement = commands.checked_update(query: query, update: update, upsert: opts.upsert, many: false, array_filters: opts.array_filters, extra: opts.statement)?
    counts = writes.send(@client.state, database: database, command: "update", collection: collection, identifier: "updates", items: [statement], ordered: true, retryable: true, extra: opts.command)?
    return ok(
        UpdateResult(matched: counts.n - counts.upserted.len(), modified: counts.modified, upserted_ids: counts.upserted)
    )

# Updates every document of `collection` that `query` matches.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `query`: the documents updated; empty for all.
# - `update`: a document of operators or an array of stages.
# - `opts`: the upsert, the array filters and the fields joined.
#
# Fails as `update_one` fails. Never retried, since it may have updated
# some. See also `update_one`.
function update_many(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, query: bson.Document, update: bson.Value, opts: WriteOptions) -> UpdateResult?
    statement = commands.checked_update(query: query, update: update, upsert: opts.upsert, many: true, array_filters: opts.array_filters, extra: opts.statement)?
    counts = writes.send(@client.state, database: database, command: "update", collection: collection, identifier: "updates", items: [statement], ordered: true, retryable: false, extra: opts.command)?
    return ok(
        UpdateResult(matched: counts.n - counts.upserted.len(), modified: counts.modified, upserted_ids: counts.upserted)
    )

# Replaces the first document of `collection` that `query` matches by
# `replacement`, its `_id` kept.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `query`: the documents considered.
# - `replacement`: the new document, with no operator: none of its fields
#   begins with `$`.
# - `opts`: the upsert and the fields joined; array filters do not apply.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `replacement` holds a field beginning with `$` or
# `opts` names array filters, and as every write fails. See also
# `update_one`.
function replace_one(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, query: bson.Document, replacement: bson.Document, opts: WriteOptions) -> UpdateResult?
    for f in replacement.fields
        if f.name.len() > 0 && f.name[0] == '$'
            return fail(
                code: INVALID,
                msg: f"a replacement holds the operator {f.name}; `update_one` applies operators"
            )

    if opts.array_filters.len() > 0
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: "a replacement takes no array filter")
    statement = commands.update_statement(
        query: query,
        update: .document(doc: replacement),
        upsert: opts.upsert,
        multi: false,
        array_filters: [],
        extra: opts.statement
    )
    counts = writes.send(@client.state, database: database, command: "update", collection: collection, identifier: "updates", items: [statement], ordered: true, retryable: true, extra: opts.command)?
    return ok(
        UpdateResult(matched: counts.n - counts.upserted.len(), modified: counts.modified, upserted_ids: counts.upserted)
    )

# Deletes the first document of `collection` that `query` matches, and
# answers how many were deleted, 0 or 1.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `query`: the documents considered; empty for any.
# - `opts`: the fields joined; `upsert` and array filters do not apply.
#
# Fails as every write fails. Retried once as a retryable write. See also
# `delete_many`.
function delete_one(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, query: bson.Document, opts: WriteOptions) -> i64?
    statement = commands.delete_statement(query: query, every: false, extra: opts.statement)
    counts = writes.send(@client.state, database: database, command: "delete", collection: collection, identifier: "deletes", items: [statement], ordered: true, retryable: true, extra: opts.command)?
    return ok(counts.n)

# Deletes every document of `collection` that `query` matches, and answers
# how many.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `query`: the documents deleted; empty for all.
# - `opts`: the fields joined.
#
# Fails as every write fails. Never retried. See also `delete_one`.
function delete_many(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, query: bson.Document, opts: WriteOptions) -> i64?
    statement = commands.delete_statement(query: query, every: true, extra: opts.statement)
    counts = writes.send(@client.state, database: database, command: "delete", collection: collection, identifier: "deletes", items: [statement], ordered: true, retryable: false, extra: opts.command)?
    return ok(counts.n)

# One write of a bulk write.
variant WriteModel
    # A document inserted, with an ObjectId where it has no `_id`.
    insert_one
        # The document.
        doc: bson.Document
    # The first document a filter matches updated.
    update_one
        # The documents considered.
        query: bson.Document
        # The operators or the stages.
        update: bson.Value
        # Whether a document is inserted where none matches.
        upsert: bool
    # Every document a filter matches updated.
    update_many
        # The documents considered.
        query: bson.Document
        # The operators or the stages.
        update: bson.Value
        # Whether a document is inserted where none matches.
        upsert: bool
    # The first document a filter matches replaced.
    replace_one
        # The documents considered.
        query: bson.Document
        # The new document.
        replacement: bson.Document
        # Whether it is inserted where none matches.
        upsert: bool
    # The first document a filter matches deleted.
    delete_one
        # The documents considered.
        query: bson.Document
    # Every document a filter matches deleted.
    delete_many
        # The documents considered.
        query: bson.Document

# What `bulk_write` answers: the counts of its writes.
record BulkWriteResult
    # The documents inserted.
    inserted: i64
    # The documents the updates and replacements matched.
    matched: i64
    # The documents they changed.
    modified: i64
    # The documents deleted.
    deleted: i64
    # The `_id`s of the documents inserted, in order.
    inserted_ids: [bson.Value]
    # The `_id`s of the documents upserts inserted.
    upserted_ids: [bson.Value]

# Runs `models` on `collection`, each run of writes of one kind sent as one
# command, and sums their counts.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `models`: the writes, in order; the empty list writes nothing.
# - `ordered`: whether the first failure stops the rest.
#
# Fails `invalid` where an update is no document of operators nor an array
# of stages, and with the first write's failure, as `insert_many` fails; the
# writes before it stand. A run holding an update or a delete of many is
# never retried. See also `insert_many`.
function bulk_write(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, models: [WriteModel], ordered: bool) -> BulkWriteResult?
    result @= BulkWriteResult(inserted: 0, matched: 0, modified: 0, deleted: 0, inserted_ids: [], upserted_ids: [])
    inserted_ids: [bson.Value] @= []
    upserted_ids: [bson.Value] @= []
    first_code: str @= ""
    first_message: str @= ""
    kinds: [i64] @= []

    for m in models
        kind_of_model = match m
            .insert_one _                                   => 0
            .update_one _ | .update_many _ | .replace_one _ => 1
            .delete_one _ | .delete_many _                  => 2

        kinds @ kinds.push(kind_of_model)

    k: i64 @= 0

    while k < models.len()
        kind = kinds[k]
        items: [bson.Document] @= []
        retryable: bool @= true

        while k < models.len() && kinds[k] == kind
            item = match models[k]
                .insert_one m =>
                    stored = writes.with_id(s: @client.state, doc: m.doc)?
                    inserted_ids @ inserted_ids.push(bson.member(stored, name: "_id")?)
                    stored
                .update_one m => commands.checked_update(query: m.query, update: m.update, upsert: m.upsert, many: false, array_filters: [], extra: bson.Document(fields: []))?
                .update_many m =>
                    retryable @ false
                    commands.checked_update(query: m.query, update: m.update, upsert: m.upsert, many: true, array_filters: [], extra: bson.Document(fields: []))?
                .replace_one m => commands.update_statement(
                    query: m.query,
                    update: .document(doc: m.replacement),
                    upsert: m.upsert,
                    multi: false,
                    array_filters: [],
                    extra: bson.Document(fields: [])
                )
                .delete_one m => commands.delete_statement(
                    query: m.query,
                    every: false,
                    extra: bson.Document(fields: [])
                )
                .delete_many m =>
                    retryable @ false
                    commands.delete_statement(query: m.query, every: true, extra: bson.Document(fields: []))

            items @ items.push(item)
            k @ k + 1

        command = if kind == 0
            "insert"
        else if kind == 1
            "update"
        else
            "delete"

        identifier = if kind == 0
            "documents"
        else if kind == 1
            "updates"
        else
            "deletes"

        match writes.send(@client.state, database: database, command: command, collection: collection, identifier: identifier, items: items, ordered: ordered, retryable: retryable, extra: bson.Document(fields: []))
            .ok counts =>
                if kind == 0
                    result.inserted @ result.inserted + counts.n
                else if kind == 1
                    result.matched @ result.matched + counts.n - counts.upserted.len()
                    result.modified @ result.modified + counts.modified

                    for id in counts.upserted
                        upserted_ids @ upserted_ids.push(id)
                else
                    result.deleted @ result.deleted + counts.n
            .err e =>
                if ordered
                    return fail(code: e.code, msg: e.msg)

                if first_code == ""
                    first_code @ e.code
                    first_message @ e.msg

    if first_code != ""
        return fail(code: first_code, msg: first_message)
    result.inserted_ids @ inserted_ids
    result.upserted_ids @ upserted_ids
    return ok(result)

## Reading

# How a query is run. `find_options` answers the defaults; a program changes
# the fields it needs.
record FindOptions
    # The fields returned, `{"name": 1, "_id": 0}`; empty for all.
    projection: bson.Document
    # The order, `{"age": -1}`; empty for the server's.
    sort: bson.Document
    # The documents passed over first, 0 for none.
    skip: i64
    # The most documents returned, 0 for no limit.
    limit: i64
    # The documents each batch holds, 0 for the server's choice.
    batch_size: i64
    # Fields joined to the `find` command: `hint`, `collation`, `comment`,
    # `maxTimeMS`, `allowDiskUse`, `let`, `min`, `max`, `returnKey`,
    # `showRecordId`, `noCursorTimeout`, `allowPartialResults`; empty for
    # none.
    command: bson.Document

# The options of a plain query: every field, the server's order, no skip, no
# limit, its batches.
function find_options() -> FindOptions
    empty = bson.Document(fields: [])
    return FindOptions(projection: empty, sort: empty, skip: 0, limit: 0, batch_size: 0, command: empty)

# A cursor of the server, read batch by batch by `next_document`. Its one
# field is the cursor's private state. A cursor that still holds documents
# on the server is closed by `close_cursor`, or by the server after ten
# minutes unused.
record Cursor
    # The cursor's state, which only this package reads and writes.
    state: cursors.Cursor

# The documents of `collection` that `query` matches, as a cursor: MongoDB's
# `find`, which a built-in's name keeps from being this function's.
#
# - `client`: the client, which the cursor's later batches are read through.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name; one that does not exist holds
#   nothing.
# - `query`: the query, `{"age": {"$gte": 18}}`; empty for all.
# - `opts`: the projection, the order, the skip, the limit, the batch's
#   size and the fields joined.
#
# Fails `invalid` where the server refuses the filter or an option, and as
# every read fails. See also `find_one`, `next_document`, `read_all`.
function find_many(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, query: bson.Document, opts: FindOptions) -> Cursor?
    body = commands.find_body(
        collection: collection,
        query: query,
        projection: opts.projection,
        order: opts.sort,
        skip: opts.skip,
        limit: opts.limit,
        batch_size: opts.batch_size,
        extra: opts.command
    )
    reply = ops.read(@client.state, database: database, body: body)?
    return ok(
        Cursor(state: cursors.of_reply(reply, database: database, batch_size: opts.batch_size, max_await_ms: 0, is_stream: false)?)
    )

# The first document of `collection` that `query` matches, in `opts`'
# order.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `query`: the query; empty for any.
# - `opts`: as `find_many` takes them; its limit is 1 whatever it says.
#
# Fails `not_found` where none matches, and as `find_many` fails. See also
# `find_many`.
function find_one(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, query: bson.Document, opts: FindOptions) -> bson.Document?
    one @= opts
    one.limit @ 1
    one.command @ commands.merged(
        base: one.command,
        extra: bson.Document(fields: [bson.Field(name: "singleBatch", value: .boolean(v: true))])
    )
    c @= find_many(@client, database: database, collection: collection, query: query, opts: one)?

    return match next_document(@client, @c)
        .ok d => ok(d)
        .err e =>
            if e.code == NOT_FOUND
                return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"no document of {database}.{collection} matches the filter")
            fail(code: e.code, msg: e.msg)

# The next document of `cursor`, the server asked for its next batch where
# the last is read.
#
# - `client`: the client the cursor was opened through.
# - `cursor`: the cursor, which this call advances.
#
# Fails `not_found` once the cursor holds no more, `timeout` where a change
# stream's wait brought no change, and as every read fails. See also
# `read_all`.
function next_document(@client: Client, @cursor: Cursor) -> bson.Document?
    return cursors.next(s: @client.state, c: @cursor.state)

# Every document `cursor` still holds, in order: the cursor read to its end.
#
# - `client`: the client the cursor was opened through.
# - `cursor`: the cursor, read to its end.
#
# Fails as `next_document` fails; a change stream's cursor fails `timeout`
# at its first wait that brings nothing. It holds every document in memory
# at once. See also `next_document`.
function read_all(@client: Client, @cursor: Cursor) -> [bson.Document]?
    return cursors.read_all(s: @client.state, c: @cursor.state)

# Tells the server to forget `cursor` where it still holds documents.
#
# - `client`: the client the cursor was opened through.
# - `cursor`: the cursor, empty after the call.
#
# Fails as every command fails. See also `find_many`.
function close_cursor(@client: Client, @cursor: Cursor) -> ()?
    return cursors.close(s: @client.state, c: @cursor.state)

# Whether `cursor` has given out every document: its batch read and the
# server holding none.
#
# - `cursor`: the cursor.
function is_exhausted(cursor: Cursor) -> bool
    c = cursor.state
    return c.id == 0 && c.at >= c.batch.len()

# How many documents of `collection` `query` matches, counted by the
# server's aggregation as the drivers count them.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `query`: the query; empty for all.
#
# Fails as every read fails. See also `estimated_document_count`.
function count_documents(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, query: bson.Document) -> i64?
    group = bson.parse_json("{\"$group\": {\"_id\": 1, \"n\": {\"$sum\": 1}}}").must()
    match_stage = bson.Document(fields: [bson.Field(name: "$match", value: .document(doc: query))])
    c @= aggregate(@client, database: database, collection: collection, pipeline: [match_stage, group], opts: aggregate_options())?

    return match next_document(@client, @c)
        .ok d => bson.int_of(bson.member(d, name: "n")?)
        .err e =>
            if e.code == NOT_FOUND
                return ok(0)
            fail(code: e.code, msg: e.msg)

# How many documents `collection` holds, as its metadata says: fast, and
# approximate after an unclean shutdown.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
#
# Fails as every read fails. See also `count_documents`.
function estimated_document_count(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str) -> i64?
    reply = ops.read(@client.state, database: database, body: bson.Document(fields: [bson.Field(name: "count", value: .text(v: collection))]))?
    return bson.int_of(bson.member(reply, name: "n")?)

# The distinct values of `field` among the documents of `collection` that
# `query` matches.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `field`: the field, a dotted path for one nested: `address.city`.
# - `query`: the query; empty for all.
#
# Fails `limit` where the values pass 16 MiB, and as every read fails. See
# also `aggregate`.
function distinct(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, field: str, query: bson.Document) -> [bson.Value]?
    body = bson.Document(
        fields: [bson.Field(name: "distinct", value: .text(v: collection)), bson.Field(name: "key", value: .text(v: field)), bson.Field(name: "query", value: .document(doc: query))]
    )
    reply = ops.read(@client.state, database: database, body: body)?
    return bson.items_of(bson.member(reply, name: "values")?)

# How a pipeline is run. `aggregate_options` answers the defaults.
record AggregateOptions
    # The documents each batch holds, 0 for the server's choice.
    batch_size: i64
    # Fields joined to the `aggregate` command: `allowDiskUse`, `maxTimeMS`,
    # `comment`, `hint`, `collation`, `let`, `bypassDocumentValidation`;
    # empty for none.
    command: bson.Document

# The options of a plain pipeline.
function aggregate_options() -> AggregateOptions
    return AggregateOptions(batch_size: 0, command: bson.Document(fields: []))

# The documents `pipeline` makes of `collection`, as a cursor.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name; "" for the database's own stages,
#   `$currentOp` or `$documents`.
# - `pipeline`: the stages, in order; one that writes (`$out`, `$merge`)
#   must stand last.
# - `opts`: the batch's size and the fields joined.
#
# Fails `invalid` where the server refuses a stage, `limit` where a stage
# passes its memory, and as every read fails. A pipeline that writes is sent
# as a write, never retried. See also `find_many`.
function aggregate(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, pipeline: [bson.Document], opts: AggregateOptions) -> Cursor?
    target: bson.Value = if collection == ""
        bson.from_int(1)
    else
        .text(v: collection)
    body = commands.aggregate(
        target: target,
        pipeline: pipeline,
        batch_size: opts.batch_size,
        extra: opts.command
    )
    writing = pipeline.len() > 0 && (bson.has_member(pipeline[pipeline.len() - 1], name: "$out") || bson.has_member(pipeline[pipeline.len() - 1], name: "$merge"))

    reply = if writing
        ops.write(@client.state, database: database, body: body, sequences: [], retryable: false)?
    else
        ops.read(@client.state, database: database, body: body)?
    return ok(
        Cursor(state: cursors.of_reply(reply, database: database, batch_size: opts.batch_size, max_await_ms: 0, is_stream: false)?)
    )

## Find and modify

# Which version of the document a find-and-modify answers.
variant ReturnDocument
    # As it was before the change.
    before
    # As the change left it.
    after

# How a find-and-modify is done. `find_and_modify_options` answers the
# defaults.
record FindAndModifyOptions
    # Which of the matches is taken, `{"age": -1}`; empty for the server's.
    sort: bson.Document
    # The fields of the document answered; empty for all.
    projection: bson.Document
    # Whether a document is inserted where none matches.
    upsert: bool
    # Which version is answered.
    returned: ReturnDocument
    # The filters of an update's `$[name]`; [] for none.
    array_filters: [bson.Document]
    # Fields joined to the command: `hint`, `collation`, `comment`,
    # `maxTimeMS`, `let`, `writeConcern`; empty for none.
    command: bson.Document

# The options of a plain find-and-modify: the document before the change.
function find_and_modify_options() -> FindAndModifyOptions
    empty = bson.Document(fields: [])
    return FindAndModifyOptions(
        sort: empty,
        projection: empty,
        upsert: false,
        returned: .before,
        array_filters: [],
        command: empty
    )

# Updates the first document of `collection` that `query` matches in
# `opts`' order, and answers it before or after.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `query`: the documents considered.
# - `update`: a document of operators or an array of stages.
# - `opts`: the order, the projection, the upsert, the version answered.
#
# Fails `not_found` where none matches and nothing is upserted, `invalid`
# where `update` is no document of operators nor an array of stages, and as
# every write fails. See also `update_one`.
function find_one_and_update(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, query: bson.Document, update: bson.Value, opts: FindAndModifyOptions) -> bson.Document?
    _ = commands.checked_update(query: query, update: update, upsert: opts.upsert, many: false, array_filters: [], extra: bson.Document(fields: []))?
    after = opts.returned == .after
    body = commands.find_and_modify(
        collection: collection,
        query: query,
        order: opts.sort,
        projection: opts.projection,
        update: update,
        return_new: after,
        upsert: opts.upsert,
        array_filters: opts.array_filters,
        extra: opts.command
    )
    reply = ops.write(@client.state, database: database, body: body, sequences: [], retryable: true)?
    return replies.modified_of(reply, what: database + "." + collection)

# Replaces the first document of `collection` that `query` matches, and
# answers it before or after.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `query`: the documents considered.
# - `replacement`: the new document, with no operator.
# - `opts`: the order, the projection, the upsert, the version answered.
#
# Fails `not_found` where none matches and nothing is upserted, `invalid`
# where `replacement` holds an operator, and as every write fails. See also
# `replace_one`.
function find_one_and_replace(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, query: bson.Document, replacement: bson.Document, opts: FindAndModifyOptions) -> bson.Document?
    for f in replacement.fields
        if f.name.len() > 0 && f.name[0] == '$'
            return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"a replacement holds the operator {f.name}")

    after = opts.returned == .after
    body = commands.find_and_modify(
        collection: collection,
        query: query,
        order: opts.sort,
        projection: opts.projection,
        update: .document(doc: replacement),
        return_new: after,
        upsert: opts.upsert,
        array_filters: [],
        extra: opts.command
    )
    reply = ops.write(@client.state, database: database, body: body, sequences: [], retryable: true)?
    return replies.modified_of(reply, what: database + "." + collection)

# Deletes the first document of `collection` that `query` matches, and
# answers it.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `query`: the documents considered.
# - `opts`: the order and the projection; the upsert and the version do
#   not apply.
#
# Fails `not_found` where none matches, and as every write fails. See also
# `delete_one`.
function find_one_and_delete(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, query: bson.Document, opts: FindAndModifyOptions) -> bson.Document?
    body = commands.find_and_modify(
        collection: collection,
        query: query,
        order: opts.sort,
        projection: opts.projection,
        update: .null_value,
        return_new: false,
        upsert: false,
        array_filters: [],
        extra: opts.command
    )
    reply = ops.write(@client.state, database: database, body: body, sequences: [], retryable: true)?
    return replies.modified_of(reply, what: database + "." + collection)

## Indexes

# An index to create: its keys and the server's options.
record IndexModel
    # The keys and their directions or kinds, in order: `{"age": 1}`,
    # `{"title": "text"}`, `{"loc": "2dsphere"}`.
    keys: bson.Document
    # The options of `createIndexes`: `name`, `unique`, `sparse`,
    # `expireAfterSeconds`, `partialFilterExpression`, `collation`,
    # `hidden`, `weights`...; empty for none, the name then made of the keys.
    options: bson.Document

# The name the server gives an index of `key_pattern` when none is asked:
# each key and its direction or kind joined by `_`, `a_1_b_-1`.
#
# - `key_pattern`: the index's keys.
#
# See also `create_index`.
function index_name_of(key_pattern: bson.Document) -> str
    return commands.index_name(key_pattern)

# Creates the index `model` describes on `collection`, and answers its name.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `model`: the keys and the options.
#
# Fails `exists` where an index of those keys exists with other options, and
# as `create_indexes` fails. An index that exists as asked is no failure.
# See also `create_indexes`, `drop_index`.
function create_index(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, model: IndexModel) -> str?
    names = create_indexes(@client, database: database, collection: collection, models: [model])?
    return ok(names[0])

# Creates every index `models` describe on `collection`, in one command, and
# answers their names in order.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `models`: the indexes; the empty list creates none.
#
# Fails `invalid` where the server refuses a key or an option, `exists` as
# `create_index` fails, and as every write fails. See also `create_index`.
function create_indexes(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, models: [IndexModel]) -> [str]?
    specs: [bson.Value] @= []
    names: [str] @= []

    for m in models
        name = match bson.member(m.options, name: "name")
            .ok v  => bson.text_of(v)?
            .err _ => commands.index_name(m.keys)

        names @ names.push(name)
        spec = commands.merged(
            base: bson.Document(fields: [bson.Field(name: "key", value: .document(doc: m.keys)), bson.Field(name: "name", value: .text(v: name))]),
            extra: m.options
        )
        specs @ specs.push(.document(doc: spec))

    if specs.len() == 0
        return ok(names)
    body = bson.Document(
        fields: [bson.Field(name: "createIndexes", value: .text(v: collection)), bson.Field(name: "indexes", value: .array(items: specs))]
    )
    _ = ops.write(@client.state, database: database, body: body, sequences: [], retryable: false)?
    return ok(names)

# The indexes of `collection`, each the server's document of its `key`,
# `name` and options.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the collection does not exist, and as every read
# fails. See also `create_index`.
function list_indexes(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str) -> [bson.Document]?
    reply = ops.read(@client.state, database: database, body: bson.Document(fields: [bson.Field(name: "listIndexes", value: .text(v: collection))]))?
    c @= cursors.of_reply(reply, database: database, batch_size: 0, max_await_ms: 0, is_stream: false)?
    return cursors.read_all(s: @client.state, c: @c)

# Removes the index named `name` from `collection`.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
# - `name`: the index's name, as `index_name_of` or `create_index` answers
#   it; `_id_` cannot be removed.
#
# Fails `not_found` where no index has the name, and as every write fails.
# See also `drop_indexes`.
function drop_index(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, name: str) -> ()?
    body = bson.Document(
        fields: [bson.Field(name: "dropIndexes", value: .text(v: collection)), bson.Field(name: "index", value: .text(v: name))]
    )
    _ = ops.write(@client.state, database: database, body: body, sequences: [], retryable: false)?
    return ok()

# Removes every index of `collection` but `_id_`.
#
# - `client`: the client.
# - `database`: the database's name.
# - `collection`: the collection's name.
#
# Fails as `drop_index` fails. See also `drop_index`.
function drop_indexes(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str) -> ()?
    return drop_index(@client, database: database, collection: collection, name: "*")

## Change streams

# How a change stream is opened. `watch_options` answers the defaults.
record WatchOptions
    # Whether an update's event holds the document: "" for the server's
    # default, `updateLookup`, `whenAvailable`, `required`.
    full_document: str
    # The token of an event the stream resumes after; empty to begin now.
    resume_after: bson.Document
    # The token of an event the stream starts after, even an invalidating
    # one; empty for none.
    start_after: bson.Document
    # How long each wait for a change lasts, in milliseconds; 0 for the
    # server's second.
    max_await_ms: i64
    # The events each batch holds, 0 for the server's choice.
    batch_size: i64

# The options of a stream that begins now.
function watch_options() -> WatchOptions
    empty = bson.Document(fields: [])
    return WatchOptions(full_document: "", resume_after: empty, start_after: empty, max_await_ms: 0, batch_size: 0)

# A change stream of `collection`, of `database`, or of the whole server: a
# cursor whose `next_document` answers each change as it comes, a document of
# its `operationType`, `documentKey`, `fullDocument`, `updateDescription`.
#
# - `client`: the client; the stream needs a replica set or a mongos.
# - `database`: the database's name; "" for every database of the server.
# - `collection`: the collection's name; "" for every collection of the
#   database.
# - `pipeline`: stages after `$changeStream`, `$match` among them; [] for
#   none.
# - `opts`: the full document, the token resumed after, the wait.
#
# Fails `unsupported` on a standalone server, and as `aggregate` fails. Its
# cursor never ends: `next_document` fails `timeout` after a wait that
# brought nothing, and the program asks again. A stream broken by a lost
# connection is not resumed for the program: `resume_token_of` gives the
# token to open another with. See also `resume_token_of`.
function watch(@client: Client, database: str, collection: str, pipeline: [bson.Document], opts: WatchOptions) -> Cursor?
    stream: [bson.Field] @= []

    if opts.full_document != ""
        stream @ stream.push(bson.Field(name: "fullDocument", value: .text(v: opts.full_document)))

    if opts.resume_after.fields.len() > 0
        stream @ stream.push(bson.Field(name: "resumeAfter", value: .document(doc: opts.resume_after)))

    if opts.start_after.fields.len() > 0
        stream @ stream.push(bson.Field(name: "startAfter", value: .document(doc: opts.start_after)))

    if database == ""
        stream @ stream.push(bson.Field(name: "allChangesForCluster", value: .boolean(v: true)))
    stages: [bson.Document] @= [
        bson.Document(fields: [bson.Field(name: "$changeStream", value: .document(doc: bson.Document(fields: stream)))])
    ]

    for p in pipeline
        stages @ stages.push(p)

    target: bson.Value = if collection == ""
        bson.from_int(1)
    else
        .text(v: collection)

    on = if database == ""
        "admin"
    else
        database
    body = commands.aggregate(
        target: target,
        pipeline: stages,
        batch_size: opts.batch_size,
        extra: bson.Document(fields: [])
    )
    reply = ops.read(@client.state, database: on, body: body)?

    wait = if opts.max_await_ms > 0
        opts.max_await_ms
    else
        1000
    return ok(
        Cursor(state: cursors.of_reply(reply, database: on, batch_size: opts.batch_size, max_await_ms: wait, is_stream: true)?)
    )

# The token after the last change a stream gave out, to open another stream
# that resumes after it.
#
# - `cursor`: a change stream's cursor.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the cursor is no change stream's or holds no token
# yet. See also `watch`.
function resume_token_of(cursor: Cursor) -> bson.Document?
    c = cursor.state

    if !c.is_stream || c.resume_token.fields.len() == 0
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: "the cursor holds no resume token")
    return ok(c.resume_token)

## Transactions

# How a transaction is run. `transaction_options` answers the defaults.
record TransactionOptions
    # The read concern's level of its reads: "" for the client's, `local`,
    # `majority`, `snapshot`.
    read_concern: str
    # The write concern of its commit; `w` "" for the client's.
    write_concern: WriteConcern
    # The longest its commit may take, in milliseconds; 0 for no limit.
    max_commit_time_ms: i64

# The options of a transaction run with the client's concerns.
function transaction_options() -> TransactionOptions
    return TransactionOptions(
        read_concern: "",
        write_concern: WriteConcern(w: "", journal: .server_default, timeout_ms: 0),
        max_commit_time_ms: 0
    )

# Starts a transaction on `client`'s session: every later command of the
# client runs in it until `commit_transaction` or `abort_transaction`.
#
# - `client`: the client; the transaction needs a replica set or a mongos.
# - `opts`: its read concern, its commit's write concern and time.
#
# Fails `invalid` where a transaction is in progress already, `unsupported`
# where the server keeps no session or is a standalone server. No command is
# sent until the first operation in it. The program retries the whole
# transaction where a failure has `LABEL_TRANSIENT_TRANSACTION_ERROR`, and
# commits again where the commit's has
# `LABEL_UNKNOWN_TRANSACTION_COMMIT_RESULT`: a function given as a callback
# could not take the client by `@`. See also `commit_transaction`.
function start_transaction(@client: Client, opts: TransactionOptions) -> ()?
    if session.in_transaction(client.state)
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: "a transaction is in progress already")
    else if !session.can_retry_writes(client.state)
        return fail(
            code: UNSUPPORTED,
            msg: f"{client.state.conn.target} keeps no session in a replica set: transactions need one or a mongos"
        )

    journal = match opts.write_concern.journal
        .server_default => ""
        .required       => "true"
        .not_required   => "false"

    concern @= client.state.settings
    concern.w @ opts.write_concern.w
    concern.journal @ journal
    concern.wtimeout_ms @ opts.write_concern.timeout_ms
    client.state.txn_number @ client.state.txn_number + 1
    client.state.transaction @ session.STARTING

    client.state.txn_read_concern @ if opts.read_concern != ""
        opts.read_concern
    else
        client.state.settings.read_concern
    client.state.txn_write_concern @ session.write_concern_of(concern)
    client.state.txn_max_commit_ms @ opts.max_commit_time_ms
    return ok()

# Commits the transaction in progress: its writes become visible together.
#
# - `client`: the client.
#
# Fails `invalid` where no transaction is in progress, and with the server's
# failure, whose labels say whether to commit again or run the whole
# transaction again; the transaction then stays in progress, to be committed
# again or aborted. A transaction that sent nothing commits at once. See also
# `abort_transaction`.
function commit_transaction(@client: Client) -> ()?
    if client.state.transaction == session.STARTING
        client.state.transaction @ session.COMMITTED
        return ok()
    else if client.state.transaction != session.IN_PROGRESS
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: "no transaction is in progress")
    fields: [bson.Field] @= [bson.Field(name: "commitTransaction", value: bson.from_int(1))]

    if client.state.txn_max_commit_ms > 0
        fields @ fields.push(bson.Field(name: "maxTimeMS", value: bson.from_int(client.state.txn_max_commit_ms)))
    _ = session.command(@client.state, database: "admin", body: bson.Document(fields: fields), kind: session.ENDS_TRANSACTION, txn: 0, sequences: [])?
    client.state.transaction @ session.COMMITTED
    return ok()

# Aborts the transaction in progress: none of its writes stands.
#
# - `client`: the client.
#
# Fails `invalid` where no transaction is in progress. The server's answer
# is not waited on for a failure, as the drivers' specification asks: a
# transaction it could not abort ends by its own time limit. See also
# `start_transaction`.
function abort_transaction(@client: Client) -> ()?
    if client.state.transaction == session.STARTING
        client.state.transaction @ session.ABORTED
        return ok()
    else if client.state.transaction != session.IN_PROGRESS
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: "no transaction is in progress")
    body = bson.Document(fields: [bson.Field(name: "abortTransaction", value: bson.from_int(1))])

    match session.command(@client.state, database: "admin", body: body, kind: session.ENDS_TRANSACTION, txn: 0, sequences: [])
        .ok _  => _ = 0
        .err _ => _ = 0

    client.state.transaction @ session.ABORTED
    return ok()

# Whether a transaction is in progress on `client`.
#
# - `client`: the client.
function is_in_transaction(client: Client) -> bool
    return session.in_transaction(client.state)

# The id of `client`'s logical session, `{"id": <UUID>}`, which every command
# carries where the server keeps sessions.
#
# - `client`: the client.
function session_id_of(client: Client) -> bson.Document
    return client.state.lsid

## Failures

# The server's own code a failure's message names, `11000` of `[11000
# DuplicateKey] ...`.
#
# - `message`: a failure's `msg`.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the message names no server code: a failure of
# this client's own. See also `has_label`.
function server_code_of(message: str) -> i64?
    return replies.code_of(message)

# The labels a failure's message names, `TransientTransactionError` among
# them, in the server's order.
#
# - `message`: a failure's `msg`.
#
# See also `has_label`.
function labels_of(message: str) -> [str]
    return replies.labels_of(message)

# Whether a failure's message names `label`.
#
# - `message`: a failure's `msg`.
# - `label`: the label, one of the `LABEL_` constants or another.
#
# See also `labels_of`.
function has_label(message: str, label: str) -> bool
    for l in replies.labels_of(message)
        if l == label
            return true

    return false

## Tests

test "options and parse_url: the defaults, a string of every option this client reads, and the refusals"
    opts = options("db.example.com")
    assert opts.retry_writes && opts.retry_reads && opts.connect_timeout_ms == 10000 && opts.mechanism == .negotiated
    full = parse_url(
        "mongodb://u:p@h:1/app?authSource=admin&authMechanism=SCRAM-SHA-1&appName=x&connectTimeoutMS=5&socketTimeoutMS=6&readPreference=nearest&readConcernLevel=majority&w=2&journal=false&wtimeoutMS=7&retryWrites=false&retryReads=false&maxPoolSize=10"
    ).must()
    assert full.mechanism == .scram_sha_1 && full.read_preference == .nearest && full.read_concern == "majority"
    assert full.write_concern == WriteConcern(w: "2", journal: .not_required, timeout_ms: 7)
    assert !full.retry_writes && !full.retry_reads && full.connect_timeout_ms == 5 && full.socket_timeout_ms == 6
    srv = parse_url("mongodb+srv://cluster0.example.com/").must()
    assert srv.srv_name == "cluster0.example.com" && srv.security == .checked(roots: "") && srv.hosts.len() == 0
    assert parse_url("mongodb://h/?authMechanism=GSSAPI").is_err()
    assert connect(
        Options(hosts: [], srv_name: "", srv_service_name: "mongodb", srv_max_hosts: 0, username: "", password: "", auth_source: "", mechanism: .negotiated, database: "", replica_set: "", direct: false, security: .plain, app_name: "", connect_timeout_ms: 1, socket_timeout_ms: 0, read_preference: .primary, read_concern: "", write_concern: WriteConcern(w: "", journal: .server_default, timeout_ms: 0), retry_writes: true, retry_reads: true, server_api: "")
    ).is_err()

test "connect: a port where nothing listens fails closed, and a port out of range invalid"
    opts @= options("127.0.0.1")
    opts.hosts @ [Host(name: "127.0.0.1", port: 1)]

    match connect(opts)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == CLOSED

    opts.hosts @ [Host(name: "127.0.0.1", port: 70000)]

    match connect(opts)
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == INVALID

test "live: CRUD, cursors across batches, counts, distinct, aggregation, find-and-modify, bulk writes, indexes and failures, against the server REBEL_MONGODB_URL names"
    match os.env("REBEL_MONGODB_URL")
        .err _ => skip_test(reason: "REBEL_MONGODB_URL names no server")
        .ok url =>
            match connect(parse_url(url).must())
                .err e => skip_test(reason: f"nothing answers: {e.code}")
                .ok first =>
                    c @= first
                    db = "rebel_test"
                    drop_database(@c, database: db).must()
                    none = bson.Document(fields: [])
                    people = [
                        bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Ada\", \"age\": 36}").must()
                        bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Grace\", \"age\": 85}").must()
                        bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Alan\", \"age\": 41}").must()
                        bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Edsger\", \"age\": 72}").must()
                        bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Barbara\", \"age\": 17}").must()
                    ]
                    ids = insert_many(@c, database: db, collection: "people", docs: people, ordered: true).must()
                    assert ids.len() == 5 && bson.kind_of(ids[0]) == "objectId"
                    dup = bson.Document(fields: [bson.Field(name: "_id", value: ids[0])])

                    match insert_one(@c, database: db, collection: "people", doc: dup)
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == EXISTS && server_code_of(e.msg).must() == 11000

                    unordered = [
                        bson.parse_json("{\"_id\": 1}").must()
                        bson.parse_json("{\"_id\": 1}").must()
                        bson.parse_json("{\"_id\": 2}").must()
                    ]
                    assert insert_many(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "unordered",
                        docs: unordered,
                        ordered: false
                    ).is_err()
                    assert count_documents(@c, database: db, collection: "unordered", query: none).must() == 2
                    by_age @= find_options()
                    by_age.sort @ bson.parse_json("{\"age\": 1}").must()
                    by_age.batch_size @ 2
                    by_age.projection @ bson.parse_json("{\"name\": 1, \"_id\": 0}").must()
                    cursor @= find_many(@c, database: db, collection: "people", query: none, opts: by_age).must()
                    assert !is_exhausted(cursor)
                    all_people = read_all(@c, @cursor).must()
                    assert all_people.len() == 5 && is_exhausted(cursor)
                    assert bson.format_json(all_people[0], mode: .relaxed).must() == "{\"name\":\"Barbara\"}"
                    assert bson.member(all_people[4], name: "name").must() == .text(v: "Grace")
                    adults = bson.parse_json("{\"age\": {\"$gte\": 18}}").must()
                    assert count_documents(@c, database: db, collection: "people", query: adults).must() == 4
                    assert estimated_document_count(@c, database: db, collection: "people").must() == 5
                    assert count_documents(@c, database: db, collection: "nothing", query: none).must() == 0
                    names = distinct(@c, database: db, collection: "people", field: "name", query: adults).must()
                    assert names.len() == 4
                    ada = find_one(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        query: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Ada\"}").must(),
                        opts: find_options()
                    ).must()
                    assert bson.member(ada, name: "age").must() == .int32(v: 36)

                    match find_one(@c, database: db, collection: "people", query: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Nobody\"}").must(), opts: find_options())
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND

                    raised = update_one(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        query: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Ada\"}").must(),
                        update: .document(doc: bson.parse_json("{\"$inc\": {\"age\": 1}}").must()),
                        opts: write_options()
                    ).must()
                    assert raised == UpdateResult(matched: 1, modified: 1, upserted_ids: [])
                    marked = update_many(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        query: adults,
                        update: .document(doc: bson.parse_json("{\"$set\": {\"adult\": true}}").must()),
                        opts: write_options()
                    ).must()
                    assert marked.matched == 4 && marked.modified == 4
                    piped = update_one(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        query: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Alan\"}").must(),
                        update: .array(items: [.document(doc: bson.parse_json("{\"$set\": {\"twice\": {\"$multiply\": [\"$age\", 2]}}}").must())]),
                        opts: write_options()
                    ).must()
                    assert piped.modified == 1
                    assert update_one(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        query: none,
                        update: .document(doc: bson.parse_json("{\"age\": 1}").must()),
                        opts: write_options()
                    ).is_err()
                    upsert @= write_options()
                    upsert.upsert @ true
                    made = update_one(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        query: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Linus\"}").must(),
                        update: .document(doc: bson.parse_json("{\"$set\": {\"age\": 55}}").must()),
                        opts: upsert
                    ).must()
                    assert made.matched == 0 && made.upserted_ids.len() == 1
                    replaced = replace_one(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        query: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Linus\"}").must(),
                        replacement: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Linus\", \"age\": 56}").must(),
                        opts: write_options()
                    ).must()
                    assert replaced.matched == 1 && replaced.modified == 1
                    assert replace_one(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        query: none,
                        replacement: bson.parse_json("{\"$set\": {}}").must(),
                        opts: write_options()
                    ).is_err()
                    assert delete_one(@c, database: db, collection: "people", query: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Linus\"}").must(), opts: write_options()).must() == 1
                    assert delete_one(@c, database: db, collection: "people", query: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Linus\"}").must(), opts: write_options()).must() == 0
                    after @= find_and_modify_options()
                    after.returned @ .after
                    older = find_one_and_update(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        query: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Grace\"}").must(),
                        update: .document(doc: bson.parse_json("{\"$inc\": {\"age\": 1}}").must()),
                        opts: after
                    ).must()
                    assert bson.member(older, name: "age").must() == .int32(v: 86)
                    before = find_one_and_update(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        query: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Grace\"}").must(),
                        update: .document(doc: bson.parse_json("{\"$inc\": {\"age\": 1}}").must()),
                        opts: find_and_modify_options()
                    ).must()
                    assert bson.member(before, name: "age").must() == .int32(v: 86)
                    swapped = find_one_and_replace(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        query: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Edsger\"}").must(),
                        replacement: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Edsger\", \"age\": 70}").must(),
                        opts: after
                    ).must()
                    assert bson.member(swapped, name: "age").must() == .int32(v: 70)
                    gone = find_one_and_delete(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        query: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Barbara\"}").must(),
                        opts: find_and_modify_options()
                    ).must()
                    assert bson.member(gone, name: "age").must() == .int32(v: 17)

                    match find_one_and_delete(@c, database: db, collection: "people", query: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Barbara\"}").must(), opts: find_and_modify_options())
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND

                    models: [WriteModel] = [
                        .insert_one(doc: bson.parse_json("{\"sku\": \"a\", \"n\": 1}").must())
                        .insert_one(doc: bson.parse_json("{\"sku\": \"b\", \"n\": 2}").must())
                        .update_many(query: none, update: .document(doc: bson.parse_json("{\"$inc\": {\"n\": 10}}").must()), upsert: false)
                        .replace_one(query: bson.parse_json("{\"sku\": \"c\"}").must(), replacement: bson.parse_json("{\"sku\": \"c\", \"n\": 0}").must(), upsert: true)
                        .delete_one(query: bson.parse_json("{\"sku\": \"a\"}").must())
                    ]
                    bulk = bulk_write(@c, database: db, collection: "items", models: models, ordered: true).must()
                    assert bulk.inserted == 2 && bulk.matched == 2 && bulk.modified == 2 && bulk.deleted == 1
                    assert bulk.inserted_ids.len() == 2 && bulk.upserted_ids.len() == 1
                    grouped = [
                        bson.parse_json("{\"$group\": {\"_id\": \"$adult\", \"n\": {\"$sum\": 1}}}").must()
                        bson.parse_json("{\"$sort\": {\"n\": -1}}").must()
                    ]
                    groups @= aggregate(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        pipeline: grouped,
                        opts: aggregate_options()
                    ).must()
                    first_group = next_document(@c, @groups).must()

                    # Edsger was replaced whole, losing his flag: three adults keep it.
                    assert bson.member(first_group, name: "n").must() == .int32(v: 3)
                    unique = IndexModel(
                        keys: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": 1}").must(),
                        options: bson.parse_json("{\"unique\": true}").must()
                    )
                    assert create_index(@c, database: db, collection: "people", model: unique).must() == "name_1"
                    assert create_index(@c, database: db, collection: "people", model: unique).must() == "name_1"

                    match insert_one(@c, database: db, collection: "people", doc: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": \"Ada\"}").must())
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == EXISTS

                    other = IndexModel(
                        keys: bson.parse_json("{\"name\": 1}").must(),
                        options: bson.parse_json("{\"unique\": false, \"name\": \"name_1\"}").must()
                    )
                    assert create_index(@c, database: db, collection: "people", model: other).is_err()
                    index_names: [str] @= []

                    for spec in list_indexes(@c, database: db, collection: "people").must()
                        index_names @ index_names.push(bson.text_of(bson.member(spec, name: "name").must()).must())

                    assert index_names == ["_id_", "name_1"]
                    drop_index(@c, database: db, collection: "people", name: "name_1").must()

                    match drop_index(@c, database: db, collection: "people", name: "name_1")
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND

                    _ = create_indexes(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        models: [unique, IndexModel(keys: bson.parse_json("{\"age\": -1}").must(), options: none)]
                    ).must()
                    drop_indexes(@c, database: db, collection: "people").must()
                    assert list_indexes(@c, database: db, collection: "people").must().len() == 1

                    match list_indexes(@c, database: db, collection: "nothing")
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND

                    copied = [
                        bson.parse_json("{\"$match\": {}}").must()
                        bson.parse_json("{\"$out\": \"people_copy\"}").must()
                    ]
                    out @= aggregate(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "people",
                        pipeline: copied,
                        opts: aggregate_options()
                    ).must()
                    assert read_all(@c, @out).must().len() == 0
                    assert count_documents(@c, database: db, collection: "people_copy", query: none).must() == 4
                    rename_collection(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        from: "people_copy",
                        to: "people_renamed",
                        drop_target: false
                    ).must()

                    match rename_collection(@c, database: db, from: "people_copy", to: "x", drop_target: false)
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND

                    create_view(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        view: "old",
                        source: "people",
                        pipeline: [bson.parse_json("{\"$match\": {\"age\": {\"$gt\": 70}}}").must()]
                    ).must()
                    assert count_documents(@c, database: db, collection: "old", query: none).must() == 1

                    create_collection(@c, database: db, collection: "people", opts: none).must()

                    match create_collection(@c, database: db, collection: "people", opts: bson.parse_json("{\"capped\": true, \"size\": 4096}").must())
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == EXISTS

                    assert list_collection_names(@c, database: db).must().len() >= 5
                    assert list_database_names(@c).must().len() >= 1

                    match run_command(@c, database: db, command: bson.parse_json("{\"noSuchCommand\": 1}").must())
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == UNSUPPORTED && server_code_of(e.msg).must() == 59

                    match find_many(@c, database: db, collection: "people", query: bson.parse_json("{\"$nonsense\": 1}").must(), opts: find_options())
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == INVALID

                    killed_opts @= find_options()
                    killed_opts.batch_size @ 1
                    killed @= find_many(@c, database: db, collection: "people", query: none, opts: killed_opts).must()
                    _ = next_document(@c, @killed).must()
                    close_cursor(@c, @killed).must()

                    match next_document(@c, @killed)
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND

                    plan = explain(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        command: bson.parse_json("{\"count\": \"people\"}").must(),
                        verbosity: "queryPlanner"
                    ).must()
                    assert bson.has_member(plan, name: "queryPlanner")
                    ping(@c).must()
                    close(@c).must()
                    assert is_closed(c) && ping(@c).is_err() && close(@c).is_err()
                    print(
                        "live: CRUD, cursors, counts, aggregation, find-and-modify, bulk writes, indexes and failures passed"
                    )

test "live: a batch past the server's most writes, sent in two commands"
    match os.env("REBEL_MONGODB_URL")
        .err _ => skip_test(reason: "REBEL_MONGODB_URL names no server")
        .ok url =>
            match connect(parse_url(url).must())
                .err e => skip_test(reason: f"nothing answers: {e.code}")
                .ok first =>
                    c @= first
                    drop_collection(@c, database: "rebel_test_batches", collection: "many").must()
                    most = server_of(c).max_write_batch_size
                    docs: [bson.Document] @= []

                    for i in range(from: 0, to: most + 1)
                        docs @ docs.push(bson.Document(fields: [bson.Field(name: "i", value: bson.from_int(i))]))

                    assert insert_many(@c, database: "rebel_test_batches", collection: "many", docs: docs, ordered: true).must().len() == most + 1
                    assert count_documents(@c, database: "rebel_test_batches", collection: "many", query: bson.Document(fields: [])).must() == most + 1
                    drop_collection(@c, database: "rebel_test_batches", collection: "many").must()
                    close(@c).must()
                    print(f"live batches: {most + 1} documents inserted in two commands")

test "live: transactions committed and aborted as a second client sees them, and a failure inside one"
    match os.env("REBEL_MONGODB_URL")
        .err _ => skip_test(reason: "REBEL_MONGODB_URL names no server")
        .ok url =>
            match connect(parse_url(url).must())
                .err e => skip_test(reason: f"nothing answers: {e.code}")
                .ok first =>
                    c @= first

                    if server_of(c).replica_set == ""
                        close(@c).must()
                        skip_test(reason: "the server is no replica set")
                    other @= connect(parse_url(url).must()).must()
                    db = "rebel_test_txn"
                    none = bson.Document(fields: [])
                    drop_collection(@c, database: db, collection: "ledger").must()
                    create_collection(@c, database: db, collection: "ledger", opts: none).must()
                    majority @= transaction_options()
                    majority.write_concern.w @ "majority"
                    majority.read_concern @ "snapshot"
                    start_transaction(@c, opts: majority).must()
                    assert is_in_transaction(c)

                    match start_transaction(@c, opts: majority)
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == INVALID

                    _ = insert_one(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "ledger",
                        doc: bson.parse_json("{\"_id\": 1}").must()
                    ).must()
                    _ = insert_one(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "ledger",
                        doc: bson.parse_json("{\"_id\": 2}").must()
                    ).must()
                    assert count_documents(@other, database: db, collection: "ledger", query: none).must() == 0
                    assert count_documents(@c, database: db, collection: "ledger", query: none).must() == 2
                    commit_transaction(@c).must()
                    assert !is_in_transaction(c)
                    assert count_documents(@other, database: db, collection: "ledger", query: none).must() == 2
                    start_transaction(@c, opts: transaction_options()).must()
                    _ = insert_one(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "ledger",
                        doc: bson.parse_json("{\"_id\": 3}").must()
                    ).must()
                    abort_transaction(@c).must()
                    assert count_documents(@other, database: db, collection: "ledger", query: none).must() == 2
                    start_transaction(@c, opts: transaction_options()).must()

                    match insert_one(@c, database: db, collection: "ledger", doc: bson.parse_json("{\"_id\": 1}").must())
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == EXISTS

                    abort_transaction(@c).must()
                    start_transaction(@c, opts: transaction_options()).must()
                    commit_transaction(@c).must()

                    match commit_transaction(@c)
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == INVALID

                    start_transaction(@c, opts: transaction_options()).must()
                    _ = insert_one(
                        @other,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "ledger",
                        doc: bson.parse_json("{\"_id\": 4}").must()
                    ).must()

                    match insert_one(@c, database: db, collection: "ledger", doc: bson.parse_json("{\"_id\": 4}").must())
                        .ok _ => assert false
                        .err e =>
                            assert e.code == EXISTS || has_label(message: e.msg, label: LABEL_TRANSIENT_TRANSACTION_ERROR)

                    abort_transaction(@c).must()
                    assert !is_in_transaction(c)
                    close(@other).must()
                    close(@c).must()
                    print("live transactions: commit, abort and a conflicting write passed")

test "live: a change stream's insert and update, and a stream resumed after its token"
    match os.env("REBEL_MONGODB_URL")
        .err _ => skip_test(reason: "REBEL_MONGODB_URL names no server")
        .ok url =>
            match connect(parse_url(url).must())
                .err e => skip_test(reason: f"nothing answers: {e.code}")
                .ok first =>
                    c @= first

                    if server_of(c).replica_set == ""
                        close(@c).must()
                        skip_test(reason: "the server is no replica set")
                    db = "rebel_test_streams"
                    drop_collection(@c, database: db, collection: "events").must()
                    create_collection(@c, database: db, collection: "events", opts: bson.Document(fields: [])).must()
                    wait @= watch_options()
                    wait.max_await_ms @ 300
                    wait.full_document @ "updateLookup"
                    stream @= watch(@c, database: db, collection: "events", pipeline: [], opts: wait).must()

                    match next_document(@c, @stream)
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == TIMEOUT

                    _ = insert_one(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "events",
                        doc: bson.parse_json("{\"_id\": 1, \"kind\": \"a\"}").must()
                    ).must()
                    inserted = next_document(@c, @stream).must()
                    assert bson.member(inserted, name: "operationType").must() == .text(v: "insert")
                    assert bson.value_at(inserted, path: "fullDocument.kind").must() == .text(v: "a")
                    token = resume_token_of(stream).must()
                    _ = update_one(
                        @c,
                        database: db,
                        collection: "events",
                        query: bson.parse_json("{\"_id\": 1}").must(),
                        update: .document(doc: bson.parse_json("{\"$set\": {\"kind\": \"b\"}}").must()),
                        opts: write_options()
                    ).must()
                    updated = next_document(@c, @stream).must()
                    assert bson.member(updated, name: "operationType").must() == .text(v: "update")
                    assert bson.value_at(updated, path: "fullDocument.kind").must() == .text(v: "b")
                    close_cursor(@c, @stream).must()
                    resumed_opts @= wait
                    resumed_opts.resume_after @ token
                    resumed @= watch(@c, database: db, collection: "events", pipeline: [], opts: resumed_opts).must()
                    again = next_document(@c, @resumed).must()
                    assert bson.member(again, name: "operationType").must() == .text(v: "update")
                    close_cursor(@c, @resumed).must()
                    close(@c).must()
                    print("live change streams: insert, update and resume passed")

test "live: logins by SCRAM-SHA-1, by SASLprep's password, refused, read-only, over TLS, and a read preference no member suits"
    match os.env("REBEL_MONGODB_URL")
        .err _ => skip_test(reason: "REBEL_MONGODB_URL names no server")
        .ok url =>
            opts = parse_url(url).must()
            wrong @= opts
            wrong.password @ "not the password"

            match connect(wrong)
                .ok _  => assert false
                .err e => assert e.code == PERMISSION && server_code_of(e.msg).must() == 18

            elsewhere @= opts
            elsewhere.replica_set @ "no-such-set"
            assert connect(elsewhere).is_err()
            secondary @= opts
            secondary.read_preference @ .secondary
            secondary.replica_set @ ""

            match connect(secondary)
                .ok got =>
                    s @= got
                    assert server_of(s).replica_set != ""
                    close(@s).must()
                .err e => assert e.code == NOT_FOUND

            match os.env("REBEL_MONGODB_SHA1_URL")
                .err _ => print("live logins: SCRAM-SHA-1 skipped, REBEL_MONGODB_SHA1_URL names no user")
                .ok sha1_url =>
                    s @= connect(parse_url(sha1_url).must()).must()
                    ping(@s).must()
                    close(@s).must()
                    forced @= parse_url(sha1_url).must()
                    forced.mechanism @ .scram_sha_256
                    assert connect(forced).is_err()

            match os.env("REBEL_MONGODB_PREP_URL")
                .err _ => print("live logins: SASLprep skipped, REBEL_MONGODB_PREP_URL names no user")
                .ok prep_url =>
                    s @= connect(parse_url(prep_url).must()).must()
                    ping(@s).must()
                    close(@s).must()

            match os.env("REBEL_MONGODB_READER_URL")
                .err _ => print("live logins: read-only skipped, REBEL_MONGODB_READER_URL names no user")
                .ok reader_url =>
                    s @= connect(parse_url(reader_url).must()).must()
                    _ = count_documents(
                        @s,
                        database: "rebel_test",
                        collection: "people",
                        query: bson.Document(fields: [])
                    ).must()

                    match insert_one(@s, database: "rebel_test", collection: "people", doc: bson.Document(fields: []))
                        .ok _  => assert false
                        .err e => assert e.code == PERMISSION && server_code_of(e.msg).must() == 13

                    close(@s).must()

            match os.env("REBEL_MONGODB_TLS_URL")
                .err _ => print("live logins: TLS skipped, REBEL_MONGODB_TLS_URL names no server")
                .ok tls_url =>
                    s @= connect(parse_url(tls_url).must()).must()
                    ping(@s).must()
                    close(@s).must()

            print("live logins: refusals, SCRAM-SHA-1, SASLprep, read-only and TLS ran")

## Examples

# The options of a server on this machine: plain TCP, the primary, no user.
test "options: a server on this machine"
    opts = options("localhost")
    assert opts.hosts == [Host(name: "localhost", port: 27017)] && opts.read_preference == .primary && opts.security == .plain

# The options a connection string names.
test "parse_url: a replica set with a user and a majority write concern"
    opts = parse_url("mongodb://app:s3cret@db1:27017,db2:27017/shop?replicaSet=rs0&w=majority").must()
    assert opts.hosts.len() == 2 && opts.username == "app" && opts.replica_set == "rs0" && opts.write_concern.w == "majority"

# A client connected, where the environment names a server; elsewhere it skips.
test "connect: a client of the server REBEL_MONGODB_URL names"
    match os.env("REBEL_MONGODB_URL")
        .err _ => skip_test(reason: "REBEL_MONGODB_URL names no server")
        .ok url =>
            c @= connect(parse_url(url).must()).must()
            assert !is_closed(c)
            close(@c).must()

# A client closed, after which every call fails `closed`.
test "close: the end of a client"
    match os.env("REBEL_MONGODB_URL")
        .err _ => skip_test(reason: "REBEL_MONGODB_URL names no server")
        .ok url =>
            c @= connect(parse_url(url).must()).must()
            c2 @= connect(parse_url(url).must()).must()
            close(@c2).must()
            assert ping(@c2).is_err()
            close(@c).must()

# Whether a client is still open.
test "is_closed: before and after close"
    match os.env("REBEL_MONGODB_URL")
        .err _ => skip_test(reason: "REBEL_MONGODB_URL names no server")
        .ok url =>
            c @= connect(parse_url(url).must()).must()
            assert !is_closed(c)
            close(@c).must()

